WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.641
On any given day,

00:00:01.641 --> 00:00:07.010
a substantial proportion of the business
news is about strategy beyond markets.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:10.590
That makes it timely, topical,
interesting, and engaging.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.177
[MUSIC]

00:00:14.177 --> 00:00:18.683
&gt;&gt; This program is about interactions with
the public, it's about interactions with

00:00:18.683 --> 00:00:23.252
government's, it's about interactions
with legal and regulatory authorities,

00:00:23.252 --> 00:00:27.899
all of which can have a profound effect on
a firm's profitability in the marketplace.

00:00:27.899 --> 00:00:32.572
&gt;&gt; It's evidence-based,
deep structural thinking that

00:00:32.572 --> 00:00:37.740
literally does not exist anywhere else,
at any other school.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:40.890
That's what's really exciting about it.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.702
&gt;&gt; The program is intended
to equip today's leaders and

00:00:43.702 --> 00:00:48.139
tomorrow's future leaders with tools that
they can implement on the day they return

00:00:48.139 --> 00:00:49.465
to their organization.

00:00:49.465 --> 00:00:55.027
&gt;&gt; New ideas, new tools, different ways of
thinking, and maybe employing new tactics,

00:00:55.027 --> 00:00:58.805
broader strategies in
the non-market-oriented realm.

00:00:58.805 --> 00:01:00.597
But more importantly than that,

00:01:00.597 --> 00:01:04.828
I'm developing a confidence around
the importance of these sorts of things.

00:01:04.828 --> 00:01:07.365
&gt;&gt; You can't sort a come up
with your business plan and

00:01:07.365 --> 00:01:10.966
then come up with your plan for
dealing with governments and the public.

00:01:10.966 --> 00:01:14.058
You need to think of those
two things at the same time.

00:01:14.058 --> 00:01:18.070
&gt;&gt; It just creates a broadening of our
traditional points of view that we

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:19.947
otherwise wouldn't have had.

00:01:19.947 --> 00:01:24.381
&gt;&gt; How an organization handles a crisis
can have short-term and long-term impact

00:01:24.381 --> 00:01:29.750
on an organization's reputation and
ability to participate in the marketplace.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.638
&gt;&gt; And if you can do a better job dealing
with those things than your competitors

00:01:33.638 --> 00:01:37.640
do, that's really a powerful foundation
for success in the marketplace.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:42.587
&gt;&gt; I feel more prepared and effective
as I return to Google to advance policy

00:01:42.587 --> 00:01:47.705
issues on the ground, as I know what
to say, when to say, and how to say it.

00:01:47.705 --> 00:01:53.468
&gt;&gt; Imagine you're a deep sea diver diving
in the dark and someone comes along and

00:01:53.468 --> 00:02:00.200
offers you an oxygen tank, a flashlight,
and a grid map of the bottom of the ocean.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.294
You liked diving, but
now you have an extraordinary opportunity

00:02:05.294 --> 00:02:09.832
to think about the world, and
understand where you are and

00:02:09.832 --> 00:02:13.639
what your choices are,
in a very different way.

00:02:13.639 --> 00:02:17.731
&gt;&gt; Participants in the program will walk
away with a set of concrete tools for

00:02:17.731 --> 00:02:18.787
how to interact and

00:02:18.787 --> 00:02:23.209
how to start strategically understand
what's going on with interest groups,

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:28.102
with activists, with governments, and with
the courts and regulatory authorities.

00:02:28.102 --> 00:02:32.246
Ways of conceptualizing what
those other folks are doing, and

00:02:32.246 --> 00:02:34.760
how to deal with them strategically.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:41.895
[MUSIC]

